Components (from Assignment 4)

CI/CD project consists of these components:

1. Pipeline Orchestrator (e.g., Jenkins / GitHub Actions runner)
2. Source Code Repository (e.g., GitHub / GitLab)
3. Build Server
4. Artifact Registry (stores build artifacts/Docker images)
5. Test Automation Server
6. Deployment Server (targets staging & production)
7. Monitoring & Failure Recovery Module
8. Notification Service (e.g., email/Slack alerts)


 Host Site

| Component | Hosting Platform |

| Pipeline Orchestrator (Jenkins) | AWS EC2 (t2.medium, Ubuntu 22.04) |
| Source Code Repository | GitHub (cloud-hosted, free/pro tier) |
| Build Server | AWS EC2 (t2.large) or GitHub Actions runner |
| Artifact Registry | Docker Hub / AWS ECR |
| Test Automation Server | AWS EC2 (t2.medium) |
| Deployment Server (Staging & Prod) | AWS EC2 separate instances |
| Monitoring & Recovery Module | AWS CloudWatch + custom Python service on EC2 |
| Notification Service | AWS SNS / Slack Webhook (fully managed, no dedicated server needed) |

Deployment Strategy

Step 1 – Server Provisioning
Launch EC2 instances on AWS (Ubuntu 22.04 LTS) for each component listed above.
Assign appropriate instance types based on load (build server gets higher CPU/RAM).
Attach Elastic IPs to Jenkins and Deployment servers for stable addressing.

Step 2 – Environment Configuration
- Install required packages on each server:
  - Jenkins server: Java JDK 17, Jenkins LTS, Git, Docker
  - Build server: Docker, Maven/Gradle, Node.js (based on project stack)
  - Test server: Selenium, JUnit, pytest (as applicable)
  - Deployment server: Docker, kubectl (if using Kubernetes), AWS CLI
- Configure environment variables (`.env` files or AWS Parameter Store) for credentials and config values.

Step 3 – Pipeline Orchestrator Setup
- Install and configure Jenkins with required plugins:
  - Git Plugin, Docker Pipeline, SSH Agent, Slack Notification Plugin
- Create a `Jenkinsfile` in the repository defining pipeline stages: `Build → Test → Artifact Push → Deploy → Monitor`
- Configure webhooks on GitHub to trigger Jenkins on every push/PR.

Step 4 – API & Inter-Component Communication Configuration
- Jenkins ↔ GitHub: GitHub webhook sends POST requests to `http://<jenkins-ip>:8080/github-webhook/` on code push.
- Jenkins ↔ Build Server: Jenkins master connects to build agent via SSH (port 22) using agent node configuration.
- Build Server ↔ Artifact Registry: Docker CLI authenticated via `docker login` pushes images to Docker Hub / AWS ECR using stored credentials.
- Jenkins ↔ Deployment Server: SSH-based deployment scripts triggered from Jenkins pipeline; uses `scp` or Ansible playbooks to deploy containers.
- Monitoring Module ↔ Deployment Server**: Monitoring script polls application health endpoint (`/health` REST API) every 30 seconds; triggers re-deployment script on failure detection.
- Monitoring Module ↔ Notification Service**: On failure, sends POST request to Slack Webhook URL or publishes to AWS SNS topic, which emails stakeholders.

Step 5 – Automated Failure Recovery Setup**
- Write a recovery script (`recover.sh`) that:
  - Detects failed container/service via health-check API
  - Automatically restarts the container: `docker restart <container_name>`
  - If restart fails 3 times, rolls back to the last stable Docker image tag from the artifact registry
- Schedule this script as a cron job or integrate it as a Jenkins post-build monitoring stage.

Step 6 – Testing the Pipeline End-to-End
- Push a dummy commit to GitHub → verify webhook triggers Jenkins build → confirm artifact pushed to registry → confirm deployment to staging server → simulate a failure and confirm auto-recovery + notification is triggered.

